# The kind lab, recorded from scratch on 2026-09-30 with APPROVE_STAGES=dev, and
# cub kubara built from the handover/approve-stages branch (0.2.4-dev), with the
# init, render and --stages fixes of the same release merged in.
#
# up.sh built a Kubara v0.16.0 platform (catalogs 3.0.0) on kind v0.31.0 with
# Kubernetes v1.35.0, on clusters named klab24-hub and klab24-spoke
# (KIND=klab24): a hub called hub in stage dev and a spoke called spoke in stage
# prod. Kubara's bootstrap installed Argo CD 3.5.2 on the hub. run.sh then ran
# against a hosted ConfigHub organization with cub v0.6.8 and
# PREFIX=klab24-0930. handover.sh installed argobot v0.1.7 on the hub.
#
# run.sh took 14 minutes. The lab directory is shown as ./kubara-lab. On kind,
# cert-manager is Degraded (Let's Encrypt refuses the example.com contact),
# traefik is Progressing (no load balancer), and the hub's argocd Application
# waits for an Ingress address.
#
# What to look for, all in section 3:
# - cub kubara handover --approve-stages dev says it approves only dev, and
#   stops before each release in prod.
# - The first handover.sh approves and publishes dev's releases, promotes
#   cert-manager, metrics-server and traefik into prod, and stops with exit
#   status 2 before it changes the hub. It prints one cub variant approve
#   command per release. Every Application still reads Git.
# - A second approval is recorded for each release, in prod. The lab has one
#   person, so run.sh runs the commands handover.sh printed.
# - The second handover.sh resumes the same change orders (the same
#   handover-...-r2 names). It skips the stage each has released, approves and
#   promotes nothing again, publishes prod, and hands the hub over: every
#   Application prunes nothing, and each reads its release from ConfigHub.
# - Sections 4 to 9 then run as in run-2026-09-30.log.
#
# A first attempt stopped at the right place, but its resumed run failed:
# promoting a change order into its last stage again is refused, once that
# stage has it ("has completed ChangeWorkflow ..., so there is nothing left to
# promote"). handover.sh now skips promoting a stage that already has the
# change order. This recording, with that fix, started from a clean
# organization on the same clusters.

