ConfigHub would hold 16 Spaces: a base Space per component, and a variant Space per cluster it runs on.
  Each base holds what Kubara generated for the earliest-stage cluster that runs it, and a rollout workflow:
  the stages above in order, each waiting for the stage ahead, and an approval attestation
  (cub variant approve) before each release. Each variant records its cluster's own render as a change.
  Secret values stay out of ConfigHub: a Secret keeps its keys and loses its values.
  One more Space, klab24-0930-kubara-generated, keeps what Kubara generated for each base, so a new
  catalog version reaches a base as one change you review.
  Kubara's hub, AppProject and ApplicationSets stay as Kubara generates them, and keep delivering
  from Git until handover, which points each ApplicationSet at the cluster's approved release instead.

Next
  cub kubara apply platform --out <dir>   # render each cluster and write apply.sh, the cub steps above

Offline: no account, no cluster, nothing changed.

== 2. Put the platform into ConfigHub: a base per component, a variant per cluster

$ cub kubara apply platform --prefix klab24-0930 --out confighub --capabilities hub=kind-klab24-hub --capabilities spoke=kind-klab24-spoke
Wrote confighub/apply.sh: 6 components, 10 variants.
Rendered hub with its own capabilities, from context kind-klab24-hub: Kubernetes v1.35.0, 168 APIs.
Rendered spoke with its own capabilities, from context kind-klab24-spoke: Kubernetes v1.35.0, 168 APIs.
These Secrets go to ConfigHub with their keys and without their values:
  argo-cd: Secret argocd/cluster-kubernetes.default.svc (3 values)
  klab24-0930-bootstrap-crds-spoke renders the same as its base, so it records no change

Next
  less confighub/apply.sh        # read what it will do
  bash confighub/apply.sh

